Description: don't ship Debian's archive trust anchors into the running system
 Upstream's `debian/debian-archive-keyring.install` installs both the
 inert keyrings and the ACTIVE trust anchors:
 .
   usr/share/keyrings/*
   etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/*
 .
 The second line drops nine `debian-archive-*.asc` files into
 `/etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/`, which apt consults at runtime as global
 trust anchors.  On Asgard that means the installed/live system would
 trust packages signed by Debian's bullseye/bookworm/trixie archive
 keys — a trust surface beyond the self-contained "apt pool = our repo
 only" policy (our mirror line already pins
 `[signed-by=athena-archive-keyring.gpg]`, so the Debian anchors are
 never needed for our pool, only a liability if any unscoped `deb`
 line were ever added).
 .
 Drop the `etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/*` install line so dpkg never owns
 those paths (no ship-then-delete; the files are simply not packaged).
 The inert `usr/share/keyrings/*` .gpg files are KEPT — they are not
 auto-trusted by apt (only consulted via explicit `signed-by=`), and
 the `-udeb` binary + debootstrap reference them by path.  Compat 10
 does not run `dh_missing`, so the now-unpackaged generated `.asc`
 files do not fail the build.
 .
 Filed as FORK-03 from the 2026-06-13 live-ISO residue audit.
Author: Harkirat S Virk <harkiratsvirk@gmail.com>
Origin: local
Forwarded: no
